        <p class="patent-number"><strong>Patent Number:</strong> CA 2,358,525</p>

        <h4>ABSTRACT</h4>
        <h2>Dynamic assignment of traffic classes to a priority queue in a packet forwarding device</h2>
        <p>This patent covers methods and systems for the dynamic assignment of traffic classes to priority queues within a packet forwarding device. The invention enables real-time, adaptive scheduling of network traffic to meet quality-of-service requirements across diverse communication networks.</p>

        <h4>Inventors</h4>
        <p>Tal Lavian, Stephen Lau</p>

        <h4>Assignee</h4>
        <p>Nortel Networks Ltd</p>

        <h4>Grant Date</h4>
        <p>April 1, 2008</p>

        <h4>FIELD</h4>
        <h3>This patent relates to packet forwarding and traffic management, and in particular to methods and systems for dynamic assignment of traffic classes to priority queues in a packet forwarding device.</h3>

        <h4>BACKGROUND</h4>
        <p>Efficient allocation and scheduling of network resources is critical for modern communication systems. As networks grow in complexity, dynamic methods for managing bandwidth, scheduling data transfers, and optimizing resource utilization become essential for maintaining quality of service and meeting diverse application requirements.</p>

        <p>Dr. Tal Lavian is a named inventor on this patent, which covers innovations in telecommunications and network communications technology. Dr. Lavian's work spans multiple areas including network architecture, security, interactive communications, frequency synthesis, and autonomous vehicle sensing systems.</p>

        <p>For more details about this patent and Dr. Lavian's extensive patent portfolio, please visit the <a href="/patents/">Patents</a> page.</p>

